{
  "case_id": "2009-08-103",
  "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Charles S. Sims",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Second Circuit's decision vacating for lack of jurisdiction a settlement agreement that compensated authors for all their arguably infringed works in the face of Congress's direction that Federal district courts shall have jurisdiction over any civil action arising under copyright is wrong for three reasons."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can -- can I ask you, one of the points made by the amicus is that, if I recall it correctly, that what -- what Congress had in mind in phrasing it this way was to enable -- enable the party who had not gone to the Copyright Office to go after dismissal on jurisdictional grounds, and the implication is that if it were not held to be jurisdictional, there would be a merits dismissal because of the failure to have gone to the Copyright Office first."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Would -- if the statute of limitations had run, could you still come back?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ginger Anders",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0000-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Section 411(a)'s registration requirement falls in the middle of those two extremes. It is not jurisdictional, but it should not be fully waivable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: So your position is that the district court really should have dismissed this case at the outset?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Record",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the works at issue were primarily already in the possession of the Library of Congress"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Library of Congress",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ANDERS: Yes, we are representing the interest of the Library of Congress."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Deborah Jones Merritt",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0001-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. JONES MERRITT: Mr. Chief Justice and may it please the Court: We will start with the statutory language as the Court has been discussing for the last half-hour."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Deborah Jones Merritt",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0001-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. JONES MERRITT: That makes -- it's a -- it's a requirement that registration be made. It is quite analogous, although stronger than the statute in the Hallstrom case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Isn't it true, though, that -- that most copyright holders, most people who write articles, freelance articles, even if it's only $65, it's not -- it's not worth it because they really don't expect to get -- they don't think anybody is going to infringe, in the first place, and if they did what establishes to be, just wouldn't be economically worthwhile? So I think it's a fact that most copyrights are not registered, isn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Jones Merritt",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. JONES MERRITT: The beauty, Your Honor, though, of the solution that Congress adopted with the registration, moving the registration to a jurisdictional element rather than to an element of the claim, as it was in the 19th century, is that the copyright owner may do this any time."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Jones Merritt",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0002-0008",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If I want to create a competing database for any of the defendants, I have to undertake the arduous work of tracking down all the owners."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Jones Merritt",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. JONES MERRITT: Because Congress wants to protect the rights of copyright owners. Congress has more than 200 years' experience balancing these two interests. And, in fact, as we speak, Congress is considering orphan works legislation to address that specific issue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Jones Merritt",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0002-0007",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. JONES MERRITT: Yes, Your Honor, they do. In order to bring any action -- if the injunction is based on infringement. So we're -- if the plaintiff brings an action for infringement and the remedy they seek is an injunction, then the copyright must be registered first."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Court issued an injunction that covered future works as well, but those were all works within the same judicial controversy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I, as appointed amicus I have argued that Section 411(a) is jurisdictional which I think the clear history and language of the statute, which I will still come back to --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Merritt",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0003-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "She relies on the Hallstrom case, but, of course, the Hallstrom case, which did avoid saying whether it was mandatory or jurisdictional, involved the enforcement of a mandatory -- at least mandatory rule, on the application of a party, and that's what the Court does, and that's why, to some extent, other than with respect to settlement agreements, this case doesn't matter a lot because the defendants will always be raising this defense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "31f6db06449527cc4f54f5b69013d79554170f29c056182b1cd72838bc0dbc95",
      "evidence_id": "ev-31f6db064495-0003-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Ms. Merritt, you were appointed by this Court as an amicus to defend the judgment below, and you have ably discharged that responsibility."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2009-08-103",
      "confidence": 0.0,
